From: Gemini PDA Linux bring-up
Subject: [PATCH] clk: mt6797: do not sweep gates inside a power domain nobody holds

#85. VENCSYS, VDECSYS and IMGSYS live inside MT6797_POWER_DOMAIN_VENC, _VDEC
and _ISP, and nothing in this kernel holds any of them -- there is no codec
driver, and the camera takes ISP only while it is open. genpd_power_off_unused()
is a late_initcall_sync exactly like clk_disable_unused() and queues one
power-off work per domain onto pm_wq, so the two run at the same instant on
different CPUs.

Whichever reaches the block first decides the boot. Measured on build #119 with
a trace that survives the reset:

  clk-trace: previous boot's sweep DID NOT FINISH after 7 entries
  clk-trace:    6 disable   venc_3            <-- NEVER RETURNED

The gate write went to an already-dark VENCSYS and never completed, with
clk_enable_lock() held and interrupts off -- so the next CPU to call
clk_enable() hard-locked too, /init never ran, and watchdog.open_timeout=120
reset the machine 120 s later. Roughly half of all reboots.

CLK_IGNORE_UNUSED makes clk_disable_unused_subtree() and
clk_unprepare_unused_subtree() both skip these gates, so the sweep never writes
those registers and the race cannot happen.

WHY NOT THE PROPER FIX. The right answer is for the provider to hold its domain,
and it is not available on this SoC yet. Merely adding `power-domains` to the
node makes platform_probe() call dev_pm_domain_attach(dev, true), which powers
the domain ON -- and scp_domain_data_mt6797[] gives VDEC, VENC and ISP no
bus_prot_mask, while MM has one and the vendor's clk-mt6797-pg.c applies TOPAXI
protection to all of them. Powering them up without that handshake hangs the
interconnect, which is the same failure by another route. Three builds tried it
and every one looped on EVERY boot: #120 (runtime PM), #122 (+ upstream's
v6.11 get-all reordering), #123 (the phandles alone, no runtime PM). Those
patches are kept at patches/v6.6/clk/*.patch.parked with the full argument in
README-parked.md.

COST: none in power. The domain is still free to be powered off, and a gate
inside an unpowered domain draws nothing whichever way it is left.

Revert this in favour of the runtime-PM patches once mtk-scpsys learns MT6797's
bus protection for these three domains -- and prove a domain can be cycled
before trusting it.

--- a/drivers/clk/mediatek/clk-mt6797-venc.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/mediatek/clk-mt6797-venc.c
@@ -18,8 +18,27 @@
 	.sta_ofs = 0x0000,
 };
 
+/*
+ * #85: these registers live inside a power domain that nothing in this kernel
+ * holds, and genpd_power_off_unused() -- a late_initcall_sync, exactly like
+ * clk_disable_unused() -- powers it off from pm_wq while the sweep is walking
+ * the tree. Whichever gets there first decides the boot: the sweep wedged
+ * gating venc_3 with the block already dark, taking two CPUs down with
+ * clk_enable_lock() held. So do not let the sweep touch them.
+ *
+ * The proper fix is for the provider to hold its domain, and it is NOT
+ * available yet: merely putting `power-domains` in the node makes
+ * platform_probe() call dev_pm_domain_attach(dev, true), which powers the
+ * domain ON -- and scp_domain_data_mt6797[] gives VDEC, VENC and ISP no
+ * bus_prot_mask, while MM has one and the vendor applies TOPAXI protection to
+ * all of them. Three builds tried it (#120, #122, #123) and every one looped on
+ * every boot. See patches/v6.6/clk/README-parked.md.
+ *
+ * Costs nothing in power: the domain is still free to be powered off, and a
+ * gate inside an unpowered domain draws nothing either way.
+ */
 #define GATE_VENC(_id, _name, _parent, _shift)				\
-	GATE_MTK(_id, _name, _parent, &venc_cg_regs, _shift, &mtk_clk_gate_ops_setclr_inv)
+	GATE_MTK_FLAGS(_id, _name, _parent, &venc_cg_regs, _shift, &mtk_clk_gate_ops_setclr_inv, CLK_IGNORE_UNUSED)
 
 static const struct mtk_gate venc_clks[] = {
 	GATE_VENC(CLK_VENC_0, "venc_0", "mm_sel", 0),
--- a/drivers/clk/mediatek/clk-mt6797-vdec.c
